<!--
    SEFCOM Oxidizer `SecurityCheckRemover` (angr
    angr/rust/optimization_passes/security_check_remover.py), ported as kuna
    `option securitycheck` (P7 edge-virtualization, DIV-82, default ON).

    rustc emits a check in front of every slice index, every string slice and
    every non-constant `/` or `%`.  Each one lowers to a conditional branch to a
    tiny block that loads the panic Location, calls one of seven Rust-only
    helpers (`core::panicking::panic_bounds_check`,
    `core::str::slice_error_fail`, `core::panicking::panic_const::*`,
    `core::slice::index::slice_*_fail`) and diverges.  Those branches double the
    CFG of ordinary Rust code and carry nothing a reader wants.

    Stage model: S7 "edge-virtualization" sub-stage, the same sub-stage and the
    same two block-graph primitives as `stackguard` (`Funcdata::removeBranch`
    plus `removeUnreachableBlocks`), on a different trigger: the callee NAME.
    All seven names are Rust-only, so the option is structurally inert on a C
    binary, which is what lets it ship default ON with no compiler-detection
    channel.  The option also asserts the divergence fact at the flow seam (the
    seven helpers are all declared `-> !` in libcore), so a helper kuna's
    address-keyed no-return discovery could not walk still produces a diverging
    panic block for the P7 edge removal to collect.

    The bytes are a hand-assembled x86-64 `fn pick(buf: &amp;[u32], idx: usize)`
    bounds check, exactly as rustc lowers it:

        100000  cmp  %rdx,%rsi          ; idx vs len
        100003  jae  10000b             ; out of range -> panic
        100005  mov  (%rdi,%rsi,4),%eax
        100008  inc  %eax
        10000a  ret
        10000b  mov  %rsi,%rdi          ; arg0 = idx
        10000e  mov  %rdx,%rsi          ; arg1 = len
        100011  lea  0x1fe8(%rip),%rdx  ; arg2 = &amp;Location (0x102000)
        100018  call 101000             ; core::panicking::panic_bounds_check
        10001d  int3

    P0 assertion: `option securitycheck on|off`.
    Pass 1 (option securitycheck off): the bug - the guard and its
      `panic_bounds_check` call are both emitted (assertions #1, #2).
    Pass 2 (default, on): the fix - neither survives; the guarded load is a
      plain statement (assertion #3), and the two assertions above stay at
      exactly one occurrence each because the second pass contributes none.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
4839d673068b04b7ffc0c34889f74889d6488d15e81f0000e8e30f0000cc
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="pick"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x101000" name="core::panicking::panic_bounds_check"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option securitycheck off</com>
  <com>lo fu pick</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option securitycheck on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
